  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 106 PM MDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nStarting the extended period Monday, 597 dam high pressure is \nforecast to be in place over the SW CONUS. As long as it remains \nsituated in that position the potential for lobes of vorticity to \nmove around the high will keep our shower and storm potential in \nplay. The current forecast for the precipitation continues to \nlook reasonable as guidance has been coming into better \nagreement with shortwave moving off of the Rockies during the \nafternoon hours on Monday and possibly an even stronger wave \nTuesday as well. Do have some concerns about Monday however as \nCAPE and shear may be a bit lacking which seems like a decent \nscenario for outflow to get out ahead of storms and they \ndissipate which which ECMWF ensemble qpf output on most members\nsuggest that this could be a realistic concern. Both may have \nthe potential for severe weather but Tuesday is currently \nforecast to have a bit better CAPE and shear. Some qpf outputs \nare also showing the potential for heavy rainfall as well so may\nneed to keep an eye on some flooding issues as well. \n\nWednesday and into the latter part of the week, guidance suggests \nthat heights rise as some ridge amplification occurs which is \nsynoptically favored to help suppress any additional storm \ndevelopment. If this signal were to change then a similar pattern to \nMonday and Tuesday would ensue and keep our daily storm chances in \nplay. \n\nHigh temperatures are continued to be forecast in the 90s for the \nextended period. 850mb moisture is forecast to remain in place \nas well which will help keep temperatures in the lower to mid \n90s instead of warming up into the upper 90s to even triple \ndigits. However if the overall high pressure were to shift \nnorthward a little more then the higher temperatures could be \nrealized.",
